PI key for non-interactive use Package information This page documents version 0.7.1 . Version 0.7.0 replaced scraperAPI with scrape-html , scrape-markdown , and get-screenshot , replaced account with status , added scrape-brand and scrape-json , renamed webSearch to web-search , and switched to a three-skill pack. See Migrating from 0.6.x . See @mrscraper/cli on npm for the current published version. Installation Install only the CLI globally: npm install -g @mrscraper/cli@latest mrscraper --version Install the CLI and the MrScraper agent skill pack for every detected harness: npx -y @mrscraper/cli@latest init --all Install for one harness: npx -y @mrscraper/cli@latest init --agent codex Supported IDs are claude-code , cursor , codex , grok , hermes , opencode , openclaw , pi , and omp . The skill pack contains three skills: mrscraper for setup and account usage, mrscraper-fetch for page retrieval, and mrscraper-serp for web search. Copyable AI setup prompt Read and follow https://github.com/mrscraper-com/cli/blob/main/skills/mrscraper/SKILL.md For an agent-controlled, non-interactive setup, skip authentication during bootstrap and start login separately: npx -y @mrscraper/cli@latest init --agent codex --yes --skip-auth mrscraper auth status --json mrscraper login CLI and skills only mrscraper init installs the CLI and skill pack. It does not install or configure MCP. See MCP Server for hosted MCP setup. init parameters Parameter Default Scope Behavior --api-key <key> — Local Saves the supplied key during bootstrap instead of browser login. --all enabled Local Installs skills for every supported harness detected on this machine. --agent <id> — Local Installs skills for one supported harness, even when its detection directory is absent. -y, --yes off Local Keeps bootstrap non-interactive. Missing authentication is left for a later login. --skip-install off Local Does not install the current CLI version globally. --skip-auth off Local Performs no authentication step. --skip-skills off Local Does not install the skill pack. --dry-run off Local Prints intended actions without installing, authenticating, or copying skills. Refresh only the skills with mrscraper setup skills . It accepts --agent <id> for one harness and --dry-run ; without --agent , it targets detected harnesses. mrscraper setup skills mrscraper setup skills --agent hermes mrscraper init --all --yes --skip-auth --dry-run Native plugins (optional) Native agent plugins are maintained separately from the CLI for ChatGPT and Codex and Claude . See Claude Code setup for installing the Claude plugin. Choose one skill installation method If npx ... init already installed the MrScraper skills for an agent, skip plugin installation for that agent. Enabling both methods can load duplicate copies of the same skills. Verifying installation After completing installation, verify that the CLI binary and commands are accessible in your environment: mrscraper --version mrscraper auth status --json mrscraper status A successful status call confirms that the CLI can load its authentication and reach the MrScraper service. Skill pack reloading Restart an agent harness or open a new terminal session after installing skills so it reloads the skill pack properly. Authentication Browser login mrscraper login The CLI opens the MrScraper login page, waits for browser approval, and saves a dedicated API key in ~/.mrscraper/auth.json . Keep mrscraper login running until approval completes. Set MRSCRAPER_HOME to change the containing directory. API-key login mrscraper login --api-key " $MRSCRAPER_API_KEY " For automation, set an environment variable instead of saving a key: export MRSCRAPER_API_KEY = "YOUR_TOKEN" Authentication precedence for service commands is: Command --token MRSCRAPER_API_KEY MRSCRAPER_API_TOKEN ~/.mrscraper/auth.json --token is sent in request headers, never in the request URL. login parameters Parameter Default Scope Behavior --api-key <key> — Local Saves this API key and does not open browser login. --token <key> — Local Deprecated alias for login's --api-key . --no-browser off Local Prompts a human for an API key; requires an interactive terminal. --no-open off Local Prints the browser sign-in URL without launching it. The CLI still waits for approval. --timeout <seconds> 180 Local Maximum time to wait for browser approval. Auth status and logout mrscraper auth status reports the credential currently available to the CLI. It reads the local configuration without sending a test request. JSON output uses the credential_configured field, plus source for an environment variable or path for a saved key: { "credential_configured" : true , "auth_type" : "api_key" , "path" : "/home/user/.mrscraper/auth.json" } mrscraper logout deletes local credential files. It does not revoke an API key remotely. Output Every service command prints a JSON envelope to stdout: { "status_code" : 200 , "data" : "<html><body>Example page content...</body></html>" , "headers" : { "content-type" : "text/html; charset=utf-8" } } status_code contains the HTTP response code. data contains the API response body. JSON bodies are parsed; other bodies, such as HTML or Markdown text, remain strings. Field names are returned as the API sends them. headers contains response headers. Credential-bearing headers are filtered, and known credential metadata in scraping and search JSON is redacted. status preserves its body fields. Failed API calls print a JSON envelope with error and exit non-zero. HTTP failures keep the status in status_code ; network failures use null . Progress messages use stderr so stdout stays machine-readable. Invalid arguments fail before any request. Save large output with shell redirection: mrscraper scrape-html https://example.com > result.json mrscraper scrape-markdown https://example.com | jq -r '.data' Response examples on this page show selected keys with placeholder values. Actual responses may contain additional fields. Environment variables Variable Default Purpose MRSCRAPER_API_KEY — Preferred API-key environment override. MRSCRAPER_API_TOKEN — Alternative API-key environment override. MRSCRAPER_HOME ~/.mrscraper Credential directory override. MRSCRAPER_API_BASE_URL https://api.app.mrscraper.com/api/v1 Development override for status and login. MRSCRAPER_FETCH_BASE_URL https://api.mrscraper.com Development override for scraping and search commands. The CLI also loads a .env file from the current working directory. Shared parameters Service options use kebab-case. Run mrscraper <command> --help to see the options a command accepts. Every service command also accepts --token <key> . Page controls scrape-html , scrape-markdown , get-screenshot , and scrape-json accept these options. Page defaults are 300 seconds, US geolocation and proxy country, real-device routing off, and dashboard saving off. Omitted retry limits mean no limit. Parameter API field Type Default Description <url> url string Required Absolute HTTP or HTTPS URL of the page to process. --timeout timeout integer 300 Page load timeout in seconds, from 1 to 300. The HTTP request allows 30 additional seconds. --geo-code geoCode string "us" Two-letter country code to load the page from, sent in lowercase. --proxy-country proxyCountry string "us" Two-letter country code of the proxy server, sent in lowercase. --super super boolean false Whether to route the request through real devices. --wait-until waitUntil string: domcontentloaded , load , networkidle0 , networkidle2 "domcontentloaded" Navigation completion event to wait for: domcontentloaded, load, networkidle0, or networkidle2. --proxy proxy string Omitted Custom proxy URL used to load the page. --max-retries maxRetries integer Omitted Maximum number of retries, as a nonnegative integer. Omit this parameter for no limit. --save-result saveResult boolean false Whether to save the result to the dashboard Result tab. Loading controls scrape-html , scrape-markdown , and get-screenshot also accept these options. scrape-json does not. Parameter API field Type Default Description --block-resources blockResources boolean false Whether to block images, fonts, and stylesheets while loading the page. --wait-for-selector waitForSelector string Omitted Wait for the page to contain the specified CSS selector before returning the result. --return-cookie returnCookie boolean false Whether to include browser session cookies in the response. Browser rendering is optional for HTML, Markdown, and JSON; screenshots always enable it. Markdown with HTML also enables browser rendering. CLI command list status Return the raw account status API response without summarizing, redacting body fields, or adding calculated fields. It takes no service parameters, and its HTTP deadline is fixed at 600 seconds. --json is accepted for compatibility; JSON is already the default. mrscraper status mrscraper status | jq '.data.data.tokenUsage' The API body contains message and data . Account details remain nested in data , including subscriptionItem and scraperAllowance , so the envelope exposes them at data.data . { "status_code" : 200 , "data" : { "message" : "Example response" , "data" : { "id" : "ACCOUNT_ID" , "tokenLimit" : 100000 , "tokenUsage" : 2500 , "stripeStatus" : "active" , "user" : { "name" : "Example user" , "email" : "user@example.com" , "isVerified" : true }, "subscriptionItem" : { "name" : "Example plan" , "tokenLimit" : 100000 }, "scraperAllowance" : { "remaining" : 97500 , "plans" : [{ "remaining" : 97500 }] } } }, "headers" : { "content-type" : "application/json; charset=utf-8" } } scrape-html Retrieve the raw HTML of a page. Browser rendering is optional. Accepts page controls , loading controls , and: Parameter API field Type Default Description --browser-rendering browserRendering boolean false Whether to render the page in a browser before returning the result. --warm-start warmStart boolean false Whether to enable warm start for more reliable scraping, at the cost of additional latency. mrscraper scrape-html https://example.com --browser-rendering --warm-start \ --wait-for-selector 'body' --timeout 120 --max-retries 0 The default response body is HTML text. Additional controls can cause the API to return JSON; the returned structure is preserved. Scrape HTML usage is based on runtime and bandwidth. Resource blocking can reduce bandwidth for text-focused pages, and --max-retries limits retry usage. See the Token Plan for the complete calculation. scrape-markdown Retrieve a page as Markdown. Including HTML also enables browser rendering and returns both outputs as JSON. Accepts page controls , loading controls , and: Parameter API field Type Default Description --browser-rendering browserRendering boolean false Whether to render the page in a browser before returning the result. --warm-start warmStart boolean false Whether to enable warm start for more reliable scraping, at the cost of additional latency. --html html boolean false Whether to include the page HTML in the response. mrscraper scrape-markdown https://example.com --html --warm-start The default response body is Markdown text. With --html , data is an object containing both outputs: { "status_code" : 200 , "data" : { "html" : "<html>...</html>" , "markdown" : "# Example page 

 ..." }, "headers" : { "content-type" : "application/json" } } get-screenshot Capture a base64 screenshot of the full page or top viewport. Browser rendering is always enabled. Accepts page controls , loading controls , and: Parameter API field Type Default Description --screenshot screenshot string: full , top "full" Screenshot area to capture: full for the entire page or top for the top viewport. --no-html html boolean true Sets html=false . Whether to include the page HTML in the response. --markdown markdown boolean false Whether to include the page Markdown in the response. mrscraper get-screenshot https://example.com --screenshot top --no-html --markdown mrscraper get-screenshot https://example.com --no-html | jq -r '.data.screenshot' | base64 -d > page.png The response body contains a base64 screenshot and the requested page outputs. The API can still include HTML when HTML output is disabled. { "status_code" : 200 , "data" : { "html" : "<html>...</html>" , "screenshot" : "BASE64_PNG_DATA" , "markdown" : "# Example page 

 ..." }, "headers" : { "content-type" : "application/json; charset=utf-8" } } scrape-brand Extract a website's colors, typography, logos, and social links as JSON. It takes only the URL; page controls are not accepted. The HTTP deadline is fixed at 120 seconds. mrscraper scrape-brand https://example.com The API body contains message and a data object with job_id , status , url , final_url , brand , and error . Brand details are at data.data.brand . { "status_code" : 200 , "data" : { "data" : { "job_id" : "JOB_ID" , "status" : "succeeded" , "url" : "https://example.com" , "final_url" : "https://example.com" , "brand" : { "name" : "Example brand" , "colors" : { "background" : "#ffffff" , "text" : "#222222" }, "typography" : { "body" : { "font_family" : "Example font" } } }, "error" : null , "bundle_url" : null }, "message" : "Example response" }, "headers" : { "content-type" : "application/json" } } scrape-json Extract structured JSON from a page using a natural-language prompt. Append Json Schema: and a JSON schema to shape the output. Accepts page controls and: Parameter API field Type Default Description --prompt prompt string "Extract all data as complete as possible." Instructions describing the data to extract. Append Json Schema: and a JSON schema to shape the output. --browser-rendering browserRendering boolean false Whether to render the page in a browser before returning the result. --html html boolean false Whether to include the page HTML in the response. --screenshot screenshot boolean false Whether to include a base64 screenshot in the response. mrscraper scrape-json https://example.com --browser-rendering \ --prompt 'Extract the first 2 items with title and price. Json Schema: {"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"title":{"type":"string"},"price":{"type":"string"}},"required":["title","price"]}}' Extraction results are in the API body's data field and follow the requested schema. Response metadata includes thread_id , token_usage , status , error , runtime , and cookies . html , markdown , and screenshot can be empty strings when not requested. { "status_code" : 200 , "data" : { "success" : true , "data" : [{ "title" : "Example item" , "price" : "10.00" }], "thread_id" : "THREAD_ID" , "token_usage" : 1 , "html" : "" , "markdown" : "" , "screenshot" : "" , "status" : "Results ready" , "error" : "" , "runtime" : 1.23 , "cookies" : [] }, "headers" : { "content-type" : "application/json" } } web-search Search the web and return ranked results with titles, URLs, descriptions, and sources. The HTTP deadline is fixed at 120 seconds. Parameter API field Type Default Description <query> query string Required Search query text, trimmed before sending, containing 1 to 1000 characters. --country country string "us" Two-letter country code to search from, sent in lowercase. --num-results numResults integer 10 Number of search results to request, as an integer from 10 to 100. Fewer results may be returned. mrscraper web-search "site:example.com documentation" --country id --num-results 20 The API body contains success , message , a data array, and tokenUsage . Each result includes rank , title , url , description , and source . { "status_code" : 200 , "data" : { "success" : true , "message" : "Example response" , "data" : [ { "rank" : 1 , "title" : "Example result" , "url" : "https://example.com" , "description" : "Example result description." , "source" : "example.com" } ], "tokenUsage" : 1 }, "headers" : { "content-type" : "application/json; charset=utf-8" } } Programmatic use The package exports the low-level functions used by the commands: scrapeHtml , scrapeMarkdown , getScreenshot , scrapeBrand , scrapeJson , webSearch , and getSubscriptionAccountApi . import { scrapeMarkdown, webSearch, getSubscriptionAccountApi } from "@mrscraper/cli" ; const token = process.env. MRSCRAPER_API_KEY ; const page = await scrapeMarkdown ({ token, url: "https://example.com" }); console. log (page.data); const results = await webSearch ({ token, query: "books" , country: "us" , numResults: 10 }); for ( const item of results.data.data) console. log (item.rank, item.title, item.url); const status = await getSubscriptionAccountApi (token); console. log (status.data.data.tokenUsage); The functions take a single options object and return the same envelope the commands print. Options use camelCase, and the JavaScript option for super is superMode . getSubscriptionAccountApi takes the token as its only argument. Programmatic calls require an explicit token and do not load saved CLI credentials or .env files. For an application client, prefer the Node.js SDK . Troubleshooting For missing credentials, run mrscraper login or set MRSCRAPER_API_KEY . For slow pages, increase --timeout up to 300 seconds. scrape-brand and web-search have fixed 120-second deadlines. Use --help on a command to inspect its accepted options. Migrating from earlier versions From 0.6.x Version 0.7.0 replaces the three 0.6.x data commands with seven service commands. The old command names are not kept as aliases. 0.6.x 0.7.x mrscraper scraperAPI <url> mrscraper scrape-html <url> mrscraper scraperAPI <url> --no-html --markdown mrscraper scrape-markdown <url> mrscraper scraperAPI <url> --markdown mrscraper scrape-markdown <url> --html mrscraper scraperAPI <url> --no-html --screenshot mrscraper get-screenshot <url> --no-html mrscraper scraperAPI <url> --home-page mrscraper scrape-html <url> --warm-start mrscraper webSearch <query> mrscraper web-search <query> mrscraper account mrscraper status scraperAPI({ token, url, ... }) scrapeHtml , scrapeMarkdown , or getScreenshot account({ token }) getSubscriptionAccountApi(token) status prints the raw API body as JSON. The 0.6.x summary at data.account , the terminal dashboard, and the --pretty and --no-color options are gone. status and web-search no longer accept --timeout . Defaults changed: page --timeout is now 300 , --geo-code and --proxy-country default to us , and an omitted --max-retries means no limit. --no-use-proxy , --bypass-proxy , --cookie-jar , --headers , --token-cap , --stream , --discard-token-secret , --no-browser-rendering , and --save are removed. Use shell redirection instead of --save . The skill pack changed from four skills ( mrscraper , mrscraper-scraper-api , mrscraper-account , mrscraper-web-search ) to three ( mrscraper , mrscraper-fetch , mrscraper-serp ). Run mrscraper setup skills to refresh installed skills. Earlier versions 0.5.x to 0.6.0: the format-specific 0.5.x commands ( scraperHtml , scraperMarkdown , getScreenshot , scraperJson , scraperMultiplePage , scraperSitemap , scrapeBrand , getBrand ) were merged into scraperAPI . Upgrade directly to the 0.7.x commands above. From 0.4.x: webSearch moved to action: "websearch" with --country and --num-results , and the MRSCRAPER_SYNC_BASE_URL setting was removed. Before 0.4.0: fetch became scraperAPI , serp became webSearch , and the original status command (with domain analytics) became account . Manage AI scrapers, reruns, and stored results in the MrScraper dashboard . Token Plan Learn how plan tokens and API tokens work in MrScraper, why token usage varies between runs, and how to create your own API token for authentication. MCP Server Official MrScraper MCP server for retrieving web pages, web search, and account usage. Connect AI clients or run the open-source server locally. On this page Requirements Installation Copyable AI setup prompt init parameters Native plugins (optional) Verifying installation Authentication Browser login API-key login login parameters Auth status and logout Output Environment variables Shared parameters Page controls Loading controls CLI command list status scrape-html scrape-markdown get-screenshot scrape-brand scrape-json web-search Programmatic use Troubleshooting Migrating from earlier versions From 0.6.x Earlier versions
